You can terminate a C program in several ways:

**1. Using the `exit()` function:**

The `exit()` function terminates the program immediately. It takes an optional argument, which is the exit status. If the argument is omitted, the exit status is 0.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main (void) {
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  exit ( 0 ) ;
}
```

**2. Using the `return` statement:**

The `return` statement terminates the program and returns a value to the operating system. This value is the exit status.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main (void) {
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  return ( 0 ) ;
}
```

**3. Using the `main()` function's return type:**

The return type of the `main()` function is int. This type can be 0 or a non-zero value. When the program terminates, the return value is the exit status.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main (void) {
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  return ( 1 ) ;
}
```

**4. Using the `abort()` function:**

The `abort()` function terminates the program immediately and prints an error message.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main (void) {
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  abort ( ) ;
}
```

**5. Using the `raise()` function:**

The `raise()` function terminates the program immediately and raises a signal.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <signal.h>

int main (void) {
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  raise ( SIGABRT ) ;
}
```

**6. Using the `atexit()` function:**

The `atexit()` function registers a function to be called when the program terminates.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void goodbye (void) {
  printf ( "Goodbye! \n" ) ;
}

int main (void) {
  atexit ( goodbye ) ;
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  return ( 0 ) ;
}
```

**7. Using the `onexit()` function:**

The `onexit()` function registers a function to be called when the program terminates.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void goodbye (void) {
  printf ( "Goodbye! \n" ) ;
}

int main (void) {
  onexit ( goodbye ) ;
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  return ( 0 ) ;
}
```

**8. Using the `setjmp()` and `longjmp()` functions:**

The `setjmp()` and `longjmp()` functions can be used to terminate the program and jump to a different point in the code.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <setjmp.h>

jmp_buf env ;

void goodbye (void) {
  printf ( "Goodbye! \n" ) ;
  longjmp ( env , 1 ) ;
}

int main (void) {
  if ( setjmp ( env ) == 0 ) {
    printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
    goodbye ( ) ;
  }
  return ( 0 ) ;
}
```

**9. Using the `signal()` function:**

The `signal()` function registers a function to be called when a signal is received. Some signals can be used to terminate the program.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <signal.h>

void goodbye (int signum) {
  printf ( "Goodbye! \n" ) ;
  exit ( 0 ) ;
}

int main (void) {
  signal ( SIGINT , goodbye ) ;
  printf ( "Hello, world! \n" ) ;
  while ( 1 ) {
    sleep ( 1 ) ;
  }
}
```
